< p: How do capital and liquidity differ? Capital acts as a financial cushion to absorb unexpected losses and generally is the difference between a firm’s assets and its liabilities. Liquidity is a measure of the ability and ease with which a firm’s assets can be converted to cash. To remain viable, a financial institution must have enough liquid assets to meet its near-term obligations, such as withdrawals by depositors and short-term debt obligations that are coming due.
